WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.240
Hello and welcome back to JD Science.

2
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.420
Today we are exploring respiration.

3
00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:08.600
Respiration releases energy from glucose.

4
00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:13.260
Aerobic respiration uses oxygen and gives carbon dioxide and

5
00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:13.600
water.

6
00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:15.940
It takes place in the mitochondria.

7
00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:20.120
When oxygen is in short supply, anaerobic respiration takes

8
00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:20.560
over.

9
00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:24.640
It releases much less energy than aerobic respiration and

10
00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:26.700
produces lactic acid in animals.

11
00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:30.640
Lactic acid builds up and causes muscle fatigue and

12
00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:31.600
an oxygen debt.

13
00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:35.060
Deep breathing after exercise repays this debt.

14
00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:39.360
In yeast and plants, anaerobic respiration produces ethanol

15
00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:40.820
and carbon dioxide.

16
00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:45.100
This is called fermentation and it is used to make bread and

17
00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:46.100
alcoholic drinks.

18
00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:50.020
During exercise, heart rate and breathing rate increase.

19
00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:54.620
Respiration is a chemical process in cells that transfers energy

20
00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:55.900
for life processes.

21
00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:59.740
Breathing is the physical re movement of air into and

22
00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:00.660
out of the lungs.

23
00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:04.960
In aerobic respiration, glucose reacts with oxygen to

24
00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:07.000
produce carbon dioxide and water.

25
00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:11.620
During intense exercise, anaerobic respiration in muscle cells

26
00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:15.320
produces lactate and releases less energy per glucose

27
00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:15.940
molecule.

28
00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:20.200
Oxygen is needed afterwards for recovery, but lactate is not

29
00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:23.040
simply the direct cause of all muscle fatigue.

30
00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:27.080
As you revise this topic, try to explain the key process

31
00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:28.200
in your own words.

32
00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:32.500
Focus on what happens, why it happens, and how the structures

33
00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:34.380
involved help the process work.

34
00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:38.140
Now pause the video and try this exam style question.

35
00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:42.860
Explain why muscles produce lactic acid during vigorous exercise.

36
00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:44.480
Here is a model answer.

37
00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:48.740
Oxygen supply cannot keep up with demand, so the muscles

38
00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:52.200
respire anaerobically, which produces lactic acid.

39
00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:56.060
If this explanation has helped, please subscribe to JD

40
00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:57.440
Science for more lessons.

41
00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:01.780
You can find further learning resources and practice questions at

42
00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:06.120
jdscience .co

43
00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:06.840
.uk.

44
00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:08.260
Thank you for watching.
